    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "the \"danger zone\" idea makes sense. but with 500 people, i'm worried about things getting chaotic during the actual service. how do i make sure the servers are actually following these protocols throughout the night and not just reading the notes once at the start? how do we keep the staff on high alert for these specific guests?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To keep your servers on high alert, you need a system that keeps the protocols front-of-mind, like a small, highly organized, and easily accessible \"Allergy Alert\" manifest for each server team. Instead of just handing them the notes, you should conduct a mandatory, brief pre-shift briefing focusing *only* on the high-risk guests and the specific allergy protocols. You can assign a dedicated \"Dietary Liaison\" server for each section of the buffet, whose sole job is to monitor those specific interactions and flag any potential issues immediately to a floor manager. This structured oversight prevents the chaos that can creep in during a large event.",
